<kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2">
  <Document>
    <name>💡 LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</name>
    <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 9:59PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 7, 2026 3:59AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</h2><p><b>Status:</b> LATEST AVAILABLE COMPOSITE (annual, hourly check)</p><p><b>Legend</b><br><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/light_pollution/legend.png?v=gibs-black-marble-2016-fdd96c57536882da-g4" width="600" alt="legend"><br>Artificial night-sky brightness on the <b>Bortle dark-sky scale Classes 1–9</b> (John E. Bortle, Sky & Telescope 2001; keyed from the NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual composite): <b>LOWEST Class 1 Excellent dark-sky site</b> near-black (NELM 7.6–8.0) &rarr; Class 2 True dark slate-gray &rarr; Class 3 Rural blue &rarr; Class 4 Rural/suburban teal-green &rarr; Class 5 Suburban olive &rarr; Class 6 Bright suburban gold &rarr; Class 7 Suburban/urban orange &rarr; Class 8 City-sky red &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ Class 9 Inner-city sky</b> near-white (NELM ≤4.0). Classes 1–5 use widely separated hues so dark skies stay readable. Class boundaries are log-spaced on the brightness index (3.2 / 7.4 / 12.8 / 19.8 / 28.8 / 40.5 / 55.5 / 74.9), shaped like the published SQM retrofit table — an approximate relative classification from composite imagery, not a calibrated SQM measurement. Same sky always shows the same class.</p><p><b>Source:</b> NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual nighttime-lights composite via NASA GIBS (Román et al., Suomi NPP VIIRS Day/Night Band)<br/><a href="https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml">https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml</a></p><p>Transparent outside valid water data so existing project layers (shipwrecks, lighthouses, harbors, parks) stay visible. Turn on/off independently of all other layers.</p><p><i>Not endorsed by NOAA.</i></p>]]></description>
    <Folder>
      <name>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<p><b>Time fetched:</b> Oct 6, 2026 9:59PM EDT<br/><b>Next update:</b> Oct 7, 2026 3:59AM EDT</p><h2>LIVE LIGHT POLLUTION</h2><p>Environmental artificial night-sky brightness around the Great Lakes from NASA's VIIRS Black Marble annual composite: a continuous surface showing where city glow and development illuminate the night most strongly and where genuinely dark skies remain. Bright cores mark metropolitan light domes; dark blues mark the darkest shorelines and waters.</p><p>Light-pollution science (a branch of environmental photometry and ecology) studies artificial skyglow from electric lighting: it disrupts circadian rhythms in wildlife and humans, wastes energy, and erases natural starlight. Satellite day/night-band radiometry separates artificial glow from natural night-sky brightness (moonlight, airglow, starlight), mapping an environmental pressure distinct from any single observatory or preserve boundary.</p><p><img src="https://wyattleathorn7.github.io/great-lakes-live-environment/light_pollution/legend.png?v=gibs-black-marble-2016-fdd96c57536882da-g4" width="600" alt="key"><br>Artificial night-sky brightness on the <b>Bortle dark-sky scale Classes 1–9</b> (John E. Bortle, Sky & Telescope 2001; keyed from the NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual composite): <b>LOWEST Class 1 Excellent dark-sky site</b> near-black (NELM 7.6–8.0) &rarr; Class 2 True dark slate-gray &rarr; Class 3 Rural blue &rarr; Class 4 Rural/suburban teal-green &rarr; Class 5 Suburban olive &rarr; Class 6 Bright suburban gold &rarr; Class 7 Suburban/urban orange &rarr; Class 8 City-sky red &rarr; <b>HIGHEST+ Class 9 Inner-city sky</b> near-white (NELM ≤4.0). Classes 1–5 use widely separated hues so dark skies stay readable. Class boundaries are log-spaced on the brightness index (3.2 / 7.4 / 12.8 / 19.8 / 28.8 / 40.5 / 55.5 / 74.9), shaped like the published SQM retrofit table — an approximate relative classification from composite imagery, not a calibrated SQM measurement. Same sky always shows the same class.<br><b>Units:</b> relative brightness index 0-100 (display; tile-background floor subtraction + skyglow bloom + square-root stretch of the published composite visualization; monotonic, ordering preserved)<br><b>Source:</b> NASA VIIRS Black Marble annual nighttime-lights composite via NASA GIBS (Román et al., Suomi NPP VIIRS Day/Night Band)<br><b>Update:</b> Composite checked hourly via tile probe; republishes only when NASA publishes a newer annual composite.<br><b>Provenance:</b> <a href="https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml">https://gibs.earthdata.nasa.gov/wmts/epsg4326/best/1.0.0/WMTSCapabilities.xml</a></p>]]></description>
